<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Asmara</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Asmara is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>, on the highland plateau at about 2,300 metres. A village grew into a city after the Italians made it the capital of their colony in the 1890s, and in the 1930s it was rebuilt with modernist and Art Deco buildings. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrean-war-of-independence">Eritrean War of Independence</a> it was an Ethiopian stronghold until 1991. Its centre is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/asmara.jpg" alt="Asmara" width="320" height="411" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tigernssnowy7 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Asmara_Montage.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…4th Brigade of the 4th Army Division at Nagelle, angry at the lack of food and water, arrested their commander; the Air Force base at Bishoftu mutinied on 12 February and the Second Division at <b>Asmara</b> on 25 February, and the protests spread to students and trade unions.</blockquote><a href="/empire/derg">Derg · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After defeating the Italians at Keren and taking <b>Asmara</b> in 1941, Britain administered Eritrea as occupied enemy territory under the laws of war.</blockquote><a href="/state/british-administration-of-eritrea">British Administration of Eritrea</a></li><li><blockquote>It received its own constitution, an elected assembly in <b>Asmara</b>, an executive and official languages, Tigrinya and Arabic, while Ethiopia controlled defence, foreign affairs, currency and trade.</blockquote><a href="/state/eritrea-federation">Eritrea (federation)</a></li><li><blockquote>Under governors such as Ferdinando Martini, the capital moved to <b>Asmara</b>, which was rebuilt with Italian modernist architecture.</blockquote><a href="/state/italian-eritrea">Italian Eritrea</a></li><li><blockquote>Mengistu fled to Zimbabwe on 21 May 1991, and the EPRDF entered Addis Ababa on 28 May, forming the Transitional Government of Ethiopia, while the EPLF took <b>Asmara</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/peoples-democratic-republic-of-ethiopia">People's Democratic Republic of Ethiopia</a></li><li><blockquote>The Eritrean People's Liberation Front took <b>Asmara</b> in May 1991, ending the war that began in 1961 as Ethiopia dismantled the Eritrea federation.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-eritrea">State of Eritrea</a></li></ul></section>
